## QA

Executable tests. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps to an
invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by test and step number.

plugin-deck is core layout infrastructure — it ships with every profile and is never toggled off.

```mdl
test QA-1: Open, resize, and close a second plank
  desc: The core deck round-trip — open a document beside an existing plank, resize the tiling split, close it.
  actors: agent
  covers: [F-1.1, F-1.6, F-3.1, T-1]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - collection: a collection in that space, to create into
    - doc1: a Markdown document named "QA Deck Doc 1" already exists and is open as the sole plank
  steps:
    - name: Create a second document
      do: In the navtree, click + on a collection and choose Markdown. Name it "QA Deck Doc 2".
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.markdown.createDraft { name: "QA Deck Doc 2", content: "# QA Deck Doc 2\n" }
      capture: created
      expect: a Document named "QA Deck Doc 2" now exists
      assert: return !!$created.object
    - name: Place it in the collection
      do: (the navtree create action places it for you)
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.addObject { object: $created.object, target: $given.collection }
      expect: "QA Deck Doc 2" appears as a row under the collection
    - name: Open it beside the first plank
      do: Click "QA Deck Doc 2" in the navtree while holding Shift.
      invoke: [op:Open] { subject: ["root/$given.space.id/content/collections/$created.object.id"], modifiers: { shift: true }, pivotId: $given.doc1.id }
      capture: opened
      expect: the deck now shows two planks, tiling side by side, "QA Deck Doc 2" alongside the first
      assert: return $opened.length > 0
      note: |
        No explicit `disposition` — the step exercises Shift forwarding (F-1.6, F-1.8): `open.ts`
        reads `input.modifiers?.shift` and forces an add regardless of disposition, so leaving
        disposition at its `solo` default and setting `modifiers: { shift: true }` is what actually
        tests the shift-forces-add rule, not a hard-coded `disposition: "add"`.
    - name: Resize the tiling split
      do: Drag the hairline divider between the two planks.
      invoke: [op:UpdateTilingSize] { size: 40 }
      expect: the split persists at the new ratio and survives a reload
  after:
    - name: Close the second plank
      do: Click the close control on "QA Deck Doc 2"'s plank.
      invoke: [op:Close] { subject: [$created.object.id] }
      expect: the deck returns to a single plank (fullbleed), "QA Deck Doc 2"'s plank is gone
    - name: Delete the second document
      do: Right-click "QA Deck Doc 2" in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [$created.object], target: $given.collection }
      expect: the row leaves the navtree
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Sidebar and companion toggles
  desc: The chrome operations outside the deck itself — sidebar visibility and the companion pane.
  actors: agent
  covers: [F-3.1, F-3.2]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
  steps:
    - name: Collapse the sidebar
      do: Click the sidebar toggle in the top-left corner.
      invoke: [op:UpdateSidebar] { state: "collapsed" }
      expect: the navtree sidebar collapses to its icon rail
    - name: Expand the sidebar again
      do: Click the sidebar toggle again.
      invoke: [op:UpdateSidebar] { state: "expanded" }
      expect: the navtree sidebar is fully visible again
    - name: Open the companion pane
      do: Click the companion toggle for the attended plank.
      invoke: [op:UpdateCompanion] { open: true }
      expect: a companion pane opens beside the attended plank
  after:
    - name: Close the companion pane
      do: Click the companion toggle again.
      invoke: [op:UpdateCompanion] { open: false }
      expect: the companion pane closes
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: The deck reports where it is scrolled as its front plank, the first one not folded with at least half its width in view, and whether it shows only its current plank, through `front` and `flat` on the layout. A follower in plugin-space mirrors a leader's deck scroll with them.
  date: 2026-11-04
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.10]
  why: The scroll offset depends on the viewport's width and the planks' sizes; the plank at the front reads the same in another deck. The fold pass already measures every plank's place in the viewport. A plank that slides off the left edge without folding leaves a sliver, so being unfolded is not enough.
  rejected: Expose the scroll offset in pixels.
```

```mdl
decision D-2: A sliding deck re-measures its front when its planks change, not only their number, so a plank replaced in place is measured, and it keeps the planks each measurement was taken among; until it has measured the planks it shows (after a plank closes, before or at the front, a reorder, or the deck starting to slide), `front` is unset rather than naming a plank it may be nowhere near. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-06
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.10]
  why: The layout is broadcast the moment it changes, before the fold pass runs, so a stale fallback reached followers as the leader's front and slid them to the first plank and back.
  rejected:
    - Name the first plank while the front is stale; the first plank can be far from where the deck is scrolled.
    - Withhold only a front plank that closed; a plank closing before it shifts the deck just as far.
```

```mdl
decision D-3: The deck's front, flatness and open companions stay deck state rather than fields on the generic layout. The deck reports its front as the space's `ViewLocation` viewport and its open companions as `AppCapabilities.Companions` view state, and plugin-space's follow shows them with the generic `LayoutOperation.ScrollIntoView` and `UpdateCompanion` (org.dxos.plugin.space#D-79); the heading wash comes from a `LayoutAccent` contribution. Replaces how [D-1] publishes the front.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.10, F-1.11, F-1.12]
  why: `front`, `flat` and companion state describe the deck, not every layout. Reporting them as view state lets the follow carry them like any other followed state, and applying them through layout operations keeps the follow free of deck code.
  rejected:
    - Generic layout fields for the front and companions; they are the deck's, not every layout's.
    - A deck `follow` that opens what the leader shows; every plugin with state that needs navigating would need its own.
    - plugin-space reading the deck's state directly; it ties follow to one layout plugin.
```

```mdl
decision D-4: `LayoutOperation.Set` takes an optional `workspace`, as `Open` does, so a follow can land each view with one `Set` or `Open` that names the space's workspace rather than a `SwitchWorkspace` before it.
  date: 2026-11-15
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.11]
  why: Two operations for one navigation race: the workspace switch seeds the space's first plank and scrolls to it while the open is still on its way. The URL a `Set` writes names its workspace, and projecting it switches the deck there.
  rejected:
    - Await `SwitchWorkspace` before opening; its seed still writes a plank and a scroll intent the follow then replaces.
```

```mdl
decision D-5: `Open`'s `variant`, when it is a linked segment (`~comments`), opens that companion beside the plank the open lands on, in the same navigation.
  date: 2026-11-06
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [op Open]
  why: Opening an object with a companion took `Open` and then `UpdateCompanion`, two navigations where `UpdateCompanion` read a deck that `Open`'s navigation may not have applied yet.
  rejected: A new `companion` field, since `variant` was unread.
```

```mdl
decision D-6: Each plank puts a scroll scope keyed by its object's EID around its article, and companions get none, so a follower restores the leader's scroll in each plank it mirrors.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.13]
  why: The plank is where the layout knows both the article and the object it shows. A companion of the same object would share the plank's position and move it.
  rejected:
    - A scope in every `Article` surface, nested ones included; an embedded article would track a second object's scroll inside the first.
```

```mdl
decision D-7: Approve [D-6].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:wittjosiah
```

```mdl
decision D-8: A companion's article sits in a scroll scope with no EID, which keeps no view state, rather than in no scope. Refines [D-6].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-1.13]
  why: Every plank renders its article the same way; leaving out the EID is enough to keep a companion from sharing or moving the plank's position.
```

---

```mdl
decision D-9: A host can show a full-width top bar that carries information and actions but no command search.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-9.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-10: The `banner` option turns the top bar on, and a `BannerEnd` surface holds the host's content. Fills in [D-9].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-9.1, F-9.2]
  why: The top bar already exists for Windows desktop; an option reuses it and a surface keeps host content out of the deck.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: In a read-only host, companions start closed.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-10.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-12: A `companionsClosed` option seeds each new workspace deck with an empty companion list, through a `NewDeck` capability that URL navigation also reads. Fills in [D-11].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-10.1]
  why: An empty list already means every companion was closed, so no new deck state is needed; a deck opened from a link is created by URL navigation and starts the same way.
```

```mdl
decision D-13: The top bar turns on when a host contributes to the banner's surfaces, with no deck option.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:wittjosiah
  supersedes: D-10
  affects: [F-9.1, F-9.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-14: The deck starts a read-only host's workspace decks with their companions closed, read from `AppCapabilities.ReadOnly` when URL navigation creates a deck, with no deck option.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:wittjosiah
  supersedes: D-12
  affects: [F-10.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-15: A `BannerStart` surface replaces the app name, so a host draws its own title, and a contribution to either banner surface turns the top bar on. Fills in [D-13].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-9.1, F-9.2]
  why: The viewer's wordmark and its font then live in the viewer, and Composer, which contributes to neither surface, keeps its bar as before.
  rejected: Turning the bar on only for `BannerEnd`: a host with only a title would get no bar.
```

```mdl
decision D-16: `DeckSchema.closedCompanionsDeck` names the deck whose companion list is empty, as opposed to absent. Fills in [D-14].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-10.1]
  why: An empty list and an absent one mean different things to the companion layout, so the closed deck gets a name instead of an inline literal.
```

```mdl
decision D-17: A link can carry an action that the app runs on the object the link opens. An action's id is a DXN, so plugins' actions do not collide.
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-18: Plugins contribute link actions as an `AppCapabilities.LinkAction` capability, and a link names one in its `action` parameter. The deck's URL projection runs it after the URL lands, activates the app's idle modules first, and removes the parameter before it runs. Fills in [D-17].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: Contributions are the only actions a link can name, so a link cannot run an arbitrary operation. The projection is what turns a URL into deck state, and it clears fullscreen when it opens a workspace, so an action that sets fullscreen has to run after it. Operation handlers load when the app goes idle, which is after boot. Removing the parameter first means a reload or a failed action never runs it again.
  rejected: A link naming an operation key directly, which would let any link run any operation; a viewer-only handler, which would not serve Composer links or Tauri deep links.
```

```mdl
decision D-19: Approve [D-18].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:wittjosiah
```

```mdl
decision D-20: A link names its action by the action's DXN without the `dxn:` prefix.
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-21: A link that runs an action opens in the state the action leaves; the reader does not see the page change into it. A public link to a presentation is presenting when it loads.
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-22: The deck contributes a `BootGate` from its startup-pass state module, pending when the boot URL names an action. The URL handler releases it when its first projection, and the action with it, has finished; the state module releases it after 10 seconds regardless. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: The URL handler waits on capabilities that arrive after the startup pass, so it activates after the boot loader would have dismissed, and a dismissed loader does not come back. The state module activates on the startup pass with only the atom registry, so the gate is decided in time. The budget keeps a projection that never finishes from holding the loader forever.
  rejected: Contributing the gate from the URL handler, which decides it too late; running the action before the projection, which clears the fullscreen state the action sets.
```

```mdl
decision D-23: The boot gate comes from its own startup-pass module, `LinkActionGate`, which also releases it after the 10-second budget; the deck's state module no longer holds it. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  supersedes: D-22
  why: The gate shares nothing with the deck's state but the startup pass, so a module of its own says why it activates there.
```

```mdl
decision D-24: The URL handler records in the deck's ephemeral state that the boot URL has been projected, and the link-action gate reads that state to release itself, as the app's other boot gates do; the gate is no longer a capability the URL handler writes.
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  supersedes: D-23
```

```mdl
decision D-25: The ephemeral field is an optional `restored` flag, set once the URL handler's first projection ends, whether or not it ran an action. The gate module keeps the 10-second budget in an atom of its own. Fills in [D-24].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: An optional field leaves every existing ephemeral state valid. The budget expiring does not mean the URL was projected, so it does not write `restored`.
```

```mdl
decision D-26: A link action runs as part of its URL's projection, so a navigation that starts before the action has run cancels it. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: An action that outlives its navigation reopens an object the reader has left, such as presenting a deck over the page they moved to.
  rejected: Checking a navigation counter before the action runs; the projection's single-flight handle already marks a navigation as overtaken.
```

```mdl
decision D-27: A link action runs only on an object its URL opened; a URL that opens nothing drops the action with a warning. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: An action is defined on the object a link opens, so with no object there is nothing for it to act on.
```

```mdl
decision D-28: Every navigation claims ownership when it starts: a URL from outside the app when it arrives, before its navigation handlers run, and an in-app navigation when its URL is pushed. An earlier link whose navigation has been claimed by a newer one does not run its action and leaves the newer URL's `action` parameter in place. Fills in [D-26].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: The single-flight projection only sees a newer navigation once that navigation projects, so a slow navigation handler would let the earlier link strip the newer URL's action and run its own over the newer page.
```

```mdl
decision D-29: A URL from outside the app is not projected when a newer navigation has claimed ownership by the time its navigation handlers finish. Its handlers still run to the end. Fills in [D-28].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: A late projection replaces the newer navigation's deck and interrupts the newer URL's projection, which cancels that URL's link action. Navigation handlers have side effects, such as accepting an invitation, that the reader asked for whichever page they end on. The boot restore is a URL from outside the app too. In Composer its handlers finish within about 100 ms of its claim, and onboarding's first in-app navigation comes 2 to 3 seconds later, so boot never overtakes its own restore.
  rejected: Interrupting the handlers when a newer navigation starts, which would drop an invitation the link carried.
```

```mdl
decision D-30: An in-app navigation that overtakes a link does not carry the link's `action` parameter into the URL it writes, so a reload does not run the action on the page the reader moved to.
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-31: An in-app navigation drops the `action` parameter from the URL it writes, and leaves it out when comparing that URL with the current one. A navigation to what the address bar already shows stays a no-op, so it neither claims ownership nor cancels the pending link. Fills in [D-30].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:wittjosiah
  affects: [F-5.5]
  why: The deck writes its URL through the same path when it re-states the deck it already shows. Counting the dropped parameter as a change would turn that write into a newer navigation and cancel the link's action.
  rejected: Having the overtaken link remove its parameter from the address bar, which cannot tell its own parameter from a newer link's.
```

## Appendix: Extension Definitions

Extension block types used in this document are defined below using
the core `ext` primitive — the only construct the base language provides.

```mdl
ext type
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0
  desc: A named data structure with typed fields and optional literals.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    fields?: FieldMap        # name[?]: TypeExpr  (# inline comment)
    literals?: UnionList     # a | b | c
    extends?: TypeRef[]
```

```mdl
ext feat
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0
  desc: A named feature grouping one or more requirements.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    req: RequirementList
  nesting: self              # feat blocks may contain feat blocks
```

```mdl
ext scenario
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0
  desc: An acceptance scenario expressed as given / when / then steps.
  fields:
    given?: Step | Step[]
    when?: Step | Step[]
    then: Step | Step[]
    tags?: TagList
```

```mdl
ext component
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0
  desc: A UI component with props, internal state, slots, actions, and events.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    props?: FieldMap            # external inputs (immutable inside component)
    state?: FieldMap            # internal reactive state
    slots?: FieldMap            # named ReactNode injection points
    actions?: ActionMap         # methods the component exposes or handles
    emits?: EventMap            # events the component raises to its parent
    layout?: CodeBlock          # ASCII sketch of visual structure (non-normative)
```

```mdl
ext op
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.op@1.0
  desc: |
    A named operation with typed inputs, outputs, and declared errors.
    Pure ops have no effects or requires. Effectful ops declare both.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    input?: FieldMap            # named input parameters
    output?: TypeExpr           # return type
    errors?: ErrorMap           # name: Prose  (when this error occurs)
    effects?: EffectList        # echo:read | echo:write | http | fs | ...
    requires?: ServiceList      # injected service dependencies
    note?: Prose                # implementation guidance (non-normative)
```
